rything that you possibly can about  that character so that you could inhabit  them in the most sort of organic way  what was your first major role and how  did you get it what do you consider  major he's like something that I got  paid for or just my first big role what  was major to you I think the first role  that I got that was major to me was I  played Reno Sweeney as a freshman and  anything goes at my at my performing  arts high school that I went to and it  was very scandalous because some  freshman got the lead role when it  should have been a senior and they they  let a freshman get the role do you have  a favorite role I don't you know people  ask me that and I really don't because I  feel like the roles you get so intimate  with a role they're like your children  you know they're all your very intimate  with them in there and they're special  to you for different reasons at  different times so I can't really pick  one that's a favorite yeah I love them  all for different reasons what is your  advice for people who want to pursue a  career that seems kind of risky like  music or acting yeah you know what's my  advice my advice is follow your heart  you know there is the old adage that if  you love what you do you'll never work a  day in your life and I think that's only  partly true because there are some days  that it does feel like work just be  willing to put in the work so don't go  into it because you want to be famous if  you're going into music or the arts as a  career go into it because you love it  because it's there there are days that  it'll be a feast and then there are just  as many and sometimes even more days  where it's famine so you better make  sure you love what you do this is more  of a personal question  but when you're at Juilliard I know that  you suffered from depression hmm would  you mind telling me kind of how you got  past that and what advice you would have  for students at MIT and other schools  who are just kind of overwhelmed exactly  yeah  I know I'm very open about what happened  to Juilliard I tried to ride to kill  myself when I was there and I was the I  you know asked for help on my student  director advisor took me and they put me  in a mental hospital which is where I  stayed for a month and got the help that  I needed I let myself get really  overwhelmed and I I don't blame myself  for that but I didn't ask for help so  what I would say to any student  is first of all it's not your fault and  what especially in high-pressure schools  like MIT and Julliard and it's servitor  situations already you know whatever it  is reach out and ask for help if it  means that you know you go to whatever  sort of mental health services they have  for you within the school or talk to a  friend or if you you know if you're  religious and you belong to sort of some  sort of house of worship ask for help  there is nothing wrong with needing help  but ask for it yeah so I guess now we're  gonna transition to some more some  lighter questions so these are kind of  rapid-fire questions okay oh god this  feels like a test it's not okay do you  have any daily rituals okay that works  what are some traditions or  superstitions you practice ooh the the  typical theatre ones you do not whistle  in a theatre you do not say the Scottish  play in a theater unless you were doing  the Scottish okay another one is I don't  I don't like to visit with people who  are attending a performance I'm doing I  do not like to visit with them before  the show but I stay on my own and very  quiet so that when I get out there I've  centered myself and I can be open and  present in the moment and I feel like  you can't do that if you're giving away  all your energy beforehand given the  choice of anyone in the world Dead or  Alive who would you want to have as a  dinner guest  only one person okay I would choose it  would be like a super boozy dinner but I  would choose Billie Holiday and Judy  Garland oh my gosh I would be the one  just trying to pick everybody up at the  end of the night but that would be an  amazing dinner party how do you relax  when I'm dry it like I'm driving home  from the studio from the set or  something like that I'm an open book so  I just I admit stuff but I listen to  forensics files the radio it's a TV show  that will play on the radio if you have  serious sex with many just plugging  things it's all these you know murders  that are that were solved by forensics  it's so it's a bit macabre but for some  reason I find it fascinating I find  those stories of how they solve all  these things  fascinating so this is kind of a fun one  but if you were an MIT student you know  what major would you want to be I can't  even answer that question because they  would never in a million years happen  nothing okay well could I study forensic  science and I mean so that I could like  you know salsa also have archaeology Oh  archaeology okay I know because I could  travel to places and do digs and stuff  okay archaeology if you had a warning  label what would it say talks too much  okay I know that you're super passionate  about LGBTQ rights do you want to tell  me a little bit more about your work in  that field I you know I've grown up in  the theater and a lot of my close  friends a lot of people in my family are  LGBTQ and you know I I was pretty vocal  in the fight for marriage equality not  only within the cut within the country  but within our state actually as well  and I also work closely with LGBTQ  homeless youth as well and my work at  the covet I'm on the board of directors  of the Covenant house which is a  homeless shelter for youth all across  the country and in Canada so we try and  be a soft landing place for them my last  question is the question that I asked to  all the guests on tea with teachers  limits if you could give any piece of  wisdom to MIT students they're just  students in general what would it be  this is what's flying into my head right  now so I'm gonna trust my gut what's  unique about how you will impact  whatever field you go into is you what  you bring that's unique about yourself  and so the Sondheim quote I then would  say would be anything you do let it come  from you then it will be new give us  more to see  thank you for watching this week's  episode of tea with teachers if you  liked what you saw subscribe and watch  more videos here follow us on social  media here if you have suggestions for  questions or professors let us know down  in the comments below have a great week
 

